ent singly. I have one each. Dragons: J1 gone, J2 and J3 remain. If I keep them, maybe Dragon Pung later? But no second copies.\n\nBest move is to shed an isolated, non-useful honor that isn't contributing to any strong pattern.\n\nAlso note: Previous player Peng'd J1 and discarded J3 \u2014 possibly getting rid of extra dragons. Across player discarded J2 earlier. So dragon danger is low.\n\nStill, **F4** is better to play than dragon tiles because:\n- F4 = North Wind = Prevalent Wind \u2192 others might want it for meld.\n- I cannot use it (no pair, not my seat wind).\n- Safer than dropping a dragon which might complete someone's Two Dragon Pungs or All Honors.\n\nAlternatively, **T9** is a terminal, possibly dangerous, and I have no other T8/T7 now (discarded T6, T7 played). No chow possible. So T9 is also a candidate.\n\nBut among all options, **B5** is weakest: only one left unseen, possibly needed by others for All Fives or Full Flush, and I can't form any chow/pung with it. But it's a middle tile, less dangerous than terminals.\n\nWait \u2014 **B6, B7, B8** was melded by across player \u2192 they had B6, B7, B8 \u2192 so B5 might complete 5-6-7 chow? They already completed the sequence, so less likely. Still, possible tenpai on 4-5-6?\n\nBetter to remove **F4** \u2014 pure wind honor, no sequence use, not part of any pair I\u2019m building.\n\nFinal consideration: Could I be heading toward **Lesser Honors and Knitted Tiles** or **Knitted Straight**? Let's check:\n\nKnitted Straight requires 1,4,7 + 2,5,8 + 3,6,9 from different suits.\n\nI have:\n- B1, B4 \u2192 good\n- T2, T5 gone (no T5), T8 available? Not in hand.\n- W3? No. Doesn't fit well.\n\nNot viable.\n\nSeven Pairs? Possible with: W1, B1, then need more pairs. I have singletons everywhere. Unlikely.\n\nSo best to clean up hand.\n\nTop discard candidates: F4 (useless wind), T9 (terminal, unsafe), B5 (middle, safer), J2/J3 (dragon, slight risk).\n\nSince F4 matches Prevalent Wind, it\u2019s potentially