 can't use it.\n\nBut let's check scoring: **Seat Wind is East (F1)**, I don't have F1. **Prevalent Wind is North (F4)**. Having a pung of F4 would give 2 points (Prevalent Wind), but I only have one.\n\nNo benefit.\n\nSo **F4** is deadwood.\n\nSimilarly, **J2** and **J3** are singleton dragons \u2014 could become Dragon Pung (2 points each) if I get three, but very slow.\n\nBetter to switch to a simpler hand.\n\nBest path: Aim for **All Simples** or **Half Flush** in Bamboo.\n\nKeep: B1, B3, B4, B5 \u2014 all useful.\n\nW1x2 \u2014 could be pair.\n\nDrop: **T9** \u2014 terminal, no support, risky.\n\nOr **F4** \u2014 honor, no support.\n\nBetween them, **F4** is worse for \"pure\" hands, so remove it.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **Play J2** or **Play J3**?\n\nJ1 is gone, so dragon set incomplete. Others may not be collecting.\n\nBut dragons are always slightly dangerous.\n\nStill, **F4** is better to drop because:\n- It\u2019s a wind, same category.\n- No role in sequences.\n- Prevalent Wind makes others collect it \u2014 but I can't stop that.\n\nBut discarding it early warns others? Too late \u2014 already seen.\n\nAnother idea: **Play W7** \u2014 isolated, not helping anything. W1x2 is pair, W7 alone.\n\nCould I form W1-2-3? No W2/W3. Discarded W5/W6/W8 \u2014 probably not building characters.\n\nSo W7 is junk.\n\nSame with T7, T9.\n\nNow comparing:\n- **F4**: honor, prevalent wind, slight risk\n- **W7**: middle suited, no connections\n- **T7/T9**: terminal or near-terminal, can complete chows\n- **J2/J3**: dragon, 2-point pattern possible\n\nBest discard: **W7**\n\nWhy?\n- Not a terminal\n- Not an honor\n- Not part of any potential sequence in hand\n- Less dangerous than T9 (which completes 789)\n- Less risky than F4 (which gives 2 points to others)\n\nW7: who needs it? For 5-6-7, 6-7-8, 7-8-9.\n\nUnseen W7: 4 total, I have one, so 3 left \u2014 plenty.\n\nAcross player discarded W9 three times \u2014 likely dumping.\n\nNext player discarded W1 \u2014 maybe not building.\n\nLow risk.\n\nSo **Play W7** is safe and removes a stranded tile.\n\nAlternatively, **Play T7** \u2014 similar logic.\n\nBut I have T2, T7, T9 \u2014 all separate.\n\nT7 could go for 5-6-7 or 7-8-9.\n\nBut T8: 4 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nStill, T7 is more connective than W7? No \u2014 no support.\n\nBut **B5** is even worse? No \u2014 B5 is part of B3-B4-B5 chow possibility.\n\nSo keep B5.\n\nBest choices: **Play W7**, **Play T7**, **Play T9**, **Play F4**\n\nAfter analysis, **Play W7** is optimal: it's a stranded middle tile in a suit I'm not developing, not terminal, not honor, minimal strategic value.\n\nReason: W7 is an isolated tile with no immediate use in forming chows or pungs, and discarding it helps streamline the hand toward potential Bamboo-based sequences (B1-B5 range) or a Seven Pairs strategy using B1 and W1. It poses minimal risk compared to terminal tiles like T9 or honor tiles that could help opponents' scoring patterns.\n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
