iscard terminals.\n- Alternatively, aim for **Outside Hand (4 points)** \u2014 requires every meld and pair to contain terminals or honors. My current B1 pung qualifies (B1 is terminal), so if I can build other melds with terminals/honors and keep pair as terminal/honor, this is viable.\n- But I lack strong draws. Looking at safe discards: W1 appears twice \u2014 others have played W1, W5, W6, W7, W8, W9 \u2014 W7 was Peng\u2019d by next player, so dangerous. But W1 only has 3 unshown \u2014 possibly some still in wall.\n- However, **F1 and F4** are both held singly. Since they are winds, and I might not get duplicates, better to discard one if not useful.\n\nBut note: I already used B1 in a pung, and opponents have many discards in W and B suits.\n\nBest chance: **reduce risk and consider safety**. Among valid actions:\n\n- Play F1: F1 is seat wind (could score 2 pts if pung), but only 2 unshown, and I\u2019ve already discarded F3 twice, F2 thrice \u2014 F2 is gone, so others may be collecting winds? Next player discarded F4 \u2014 so maybe not valuing it. Still, **discarding F1 risks someone using it for Prevalent/Seat Wind pung** \u2014 but I can't use it myself without duplicate.\n- Play F4: prevalent wind (North), same risk.\n- Play J2/J3: dragon tiles \u2014 valuable for patterns like Two Dragon Pungs, All Honors, etc. But J1 is gone (all 4 used), so Red Dragon out. J2 and J3 still have 3 each unshown \u2014 possible. But holding both might help form pairs.\n- Play B2: only 1 left unshown \u2014 very low chance to draw again. B2 is terminal, part of Outside Hand possibility. But currently isolated.\n- Play W2: I have two W1s and one W2 \u2014 this is part of a potential Chow [W1,W2,W3]. Discarding W2 breaks that possibility. Risky.\n- Play T7/T2/T9: T2 is already exposed in a chow by opponent (T2,T3,T4), so discard may be dangerous. T7 and T9 are terminals, scattered.\n- Play W1: I have two \u2014 safest among W1s? But breaks potential chow.\n- Play J3: similar to J2.\n\nWait: I have **two W1s and one W2** \u2014 that's close to a chow. If I keep them, and draw W3 (4 unshown), I can complete [W1,W2,W3]. That would give:\n- Short Straight (if another chow adjacent) \u2014 but not yet.\n- Mixed Double Chow (1 pt) if I get two identical chows later \u2014 unlikely.\nBut currently, more valuable to preserve shape.\n\nHowever, **I must discard**. Best candidate: **discard a less useful honor**.\n\nCompare F1 vs F4:\n- Seat Wind = East = F1 \u2192 pung of F1 gives 2 pts.\n- Prevailing Wind = North = F4 \u2192 pung of F4 gives 2 pts.\n- I have only one of each. Unshown: F1:2, F3:2, F4:2 \u2014 so duplicates possible, but unlikely soon.\n- Opponents have discarded both F1 and F4 multiple times \u2014 seems they don\u2019t value them.\n- Previous player discarded F2, F3, F4 \u2014 so probably doesn\u2019t have F4 pair.\n- But still, **F1 and F4 are equally weak to hold**.\n\nNow look at **J2 and J3**: both dragons. Holding both gives potential for **Two Dragon Pungs** (6 pts) if I can make pungs. J2 and J3 each have 3 unshown \u2014 possible. But I only have one each now.\n\nAlternatively, **B2**: only 1 left \u2014 can't form pung easily. Useless.\n\nT7, T9: isolated terminals.\n\nSo the **least damaging discard** is likely **T9** \u2014 it's a terminal, not part of any clear sequence, and I have no other T8 or T7 to support chow. T7 is there, but T7,T8,T9 chow needs T8 \u2014 T8 has 2 unshown, but across player has T8 in chow \u2014 so possibly dangerous. And I have only one T7.\n\nSimilarly, **T2** is dangerous \u2014 across player just completed T2,T3,T4 chow via Chi \u2014 so they have T2 \u2014 discarding T2 might let them win.\n\nSo **do NOT discard T2**.\n\nT9: less likely to be needed \u2014 no one has shown interest in high T tiles. Across has B6-B8, T2-T4 \u2014 not high T. Others haven't played many T.\n\nThus, **T9 is safer**.\n\nBut wait \u2014 **J3**: White Dragon. Is it safer than T9?\n\nJ3 has 3 unshown \u2014 could be useful. But if I discard it, and someone wins, I lose big. But same for T9.\n\nBut **dragon pungs are more valuable** \u2014 Dragon Pung is 2 pts, while T9 pung is only 1 pt (Pung of Terminals or Honors).\n\nSo better to keep J3 for potential dragon pattern.\n\nSame with J2.\n\nSo prioritize keeping **dragons** over terminals.\n\nTherefore, better to discard **T9** than J2 or J3.\n\nNow between **F1 and F4** \u2014 same logic.\n\nBut **F1 is my seat wind** \u2014 slightly more valuable if I ever get a pair. Same for F4.\n\nBut I have no pair.\n\nStill, discarding **a wind** is risky because of 2-pt patterns.\n\nBut so is discarding **terminal**.\n\nAlternative idea: **Play W2**?\n\nI have two W1s and one W2 \u2014 if I discard W2, I break the potential chow. But I still have two W1s \u2014 could form pair or even pung if I draw W1 (3 unshown). But W1 has been played by others \u2014 next player played W1, so they may have interest.\n\nBut W1 is also terminal \u2014 part of Outside Hand.\n\nBut **keeping two W1s** allows for pair + possible pung.\n\nDiscarding **one W1** would be better? But I have two \u2014 discarding one keeps a single, which is worse.\n\nWait \u2014 valid actions include **Play W1** \u2014 yes.\n\nSo options: Play W1, Play W2, Play T9, etc.\n\nBest move: **discard a tile that doesn't contribute to structure and is least dangerous**.\n\nLet\u2019s assess **B2**: only 1 left, I have it, so no one else can make pung. Safe to discard? Possibly. But B2 is terminal \u2014 part of Outside Hand. But I already have B1 pung (valid), so if I keep B2, it\u2019s not helping.\n\nBut B2 could be used in chow? B1,B2,B3 \u2014 but B1 is already in pung, B3 only 1 left \u2014 very unlikely.\n\nSo B2 is useless.\n\nSimilarly, **T7**: only T7 \u2014 need T6/T8 for chow. T6:3 unshown, T8:2 \u2014 possible, but I have no other T.\n\nSo T7 is weak.\n\nBut **T9** is weaker \u2014 no chow possible without T7,T8 or T8,T9,T? No.\n\nT7,T8,T9 chow \u2014 I have T7 and T9 \u2014 missing T8.\n\nT8 has 2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nSo **T7 and T9 together** can form base for chow if I draw T8.\n\nWhereas B2 is isolated.\n\nSo **B2 is the weakest tile**.\n\nAlso, B1, B3, B4, B6, B9 are all heavily used \u2014 B1 pung, B4 pung, B6-B8 chow \u2014 so B2 is surrounded by danger \u2014 someone may be waiting for B2 to complete chow like B1,B2,B3.\n\nBut B1 is already in my pung \u2014 so B1,B2,B3 cannot be formed by others \u2014 only me, and I can't because I have no B3 (only 1 left).\n\nSo **B2 is safe to discard** \u2014 no one can use it for chow involving B1.\n\nCould someone have B2,B3,B4? B4 is in previous player\u2019s pung \u2014 so they have three B4s \u2014 possibly discarded B3 \u2014 they played B3 twice \u2014 so they had at least two B3s \u2014 but B3 only 1 unshown \u2014 likely gone.\n\nSo B2,B3,B4 also blocked.\n\nThus, **B2 is safe**.\n\nMoreover, I have only one B2 \u2014 no pair, no future.\n\nSo **Play B2** is the safest and most logical discard.\n\nIt doesn\u2019t break any promising structure, and it\u2019s unlikely to feed an opponent.\n\nCompare to discarding W1: I have two \u2014 discarding one leaves one, breaking potential pair. Worse.\n\nDiscarding T9: breaks potential T7,T8,T9 chow \u2014 which is possible if I draw T8.\n\nSo **B2 > T9 > others**.\n\nFinal decision: **Play B2**\n\nReason: B2 is an isolated terminal tile with limited utility in forming chows due to the unavailability of complementary tiles (B1 is already used in my pung, B3 and B4 are scarce). It poses minimal risk of feeding opponents' winning hands, as critical sequences like B1-B2-B3 or B2-B3-B4 are blocked by existing melds and discards. Retaining more flexible tiles like T7/T9 (potential for T7-T8-T9 chow) or honors (potential for wind/dragon patterns) is preferable. Thus, discarding B2 minimizes risk while preserving strategic options.\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
